  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 207 AM CDT Sat Sep 12 2026\n\n...This Weekend...\nThis morning a closed upper low is pushing east across the mid \nMississippi Valley just south of Illinois. It is expected to \nopen up as it drifts eastward through the lower Ohio Valley this\nmorning. A few isolated light showers or storms will be \npossible with this feature east of I-57 this morning, but most \nof central Illinois is expected to stay dry today. \n\nTo the northwest, a cold front is dropping across portions of \nthe Upper Midwest and central Great Plains this morning. Ahead \nof the front, a thermal ridge, with 850mb temperatures reaching\naround 18C, will push into central Illinois. This will allow \ntemperatures to rebound this afternoon, with widespread highs \ninto the upper 80s, and a few spots touching 90.\n\nAs the front moves into and eventually stalls over the area on \nSunday, a sharp temperature gradient will form across the \nregion. Highs north of the Illinois River are projected to top \nout in the mid 70s, whereas areas south of I-70 will warm to \naround 90. \n\nA prominent 700/750mb warm nose will keep deep surface based \nconvection capped Sunday, however, some light precipitation \nstill appears possible. Forcing is quite nebulous Sunday, but \nweak isentropic ascent eventually develops along the 310-315K \nsurfaces resulting in mid level saturation that could produce a \nfew light showers or sprinkles. NBM mean 24-hour QPF for Sunday \ngenerally shows up to a third of an inch in the west to 0.15 \ninches in the east. \n\n...Monday through Wednesday...\nAn active, unsettled pattern is expected during the first half \nof next week. A high-amplitude upper wave is forecast to move \nfrom the Pacific Northwest across the Canadian Rockies Sunday \nnight into Monday. A deep surface low will develop in response \nand track from the Canadian Prairies toward Hudson Bay Monday \ninto Tuesday. \n\nAn associated warm front will advance across the Great Plains \nSunday night, triggering nocturnal convection. Some of this \ndecaying activity could reach parts of west central Illinois as \nthe warm front shifts northward through the area on Monday. \n\nA better chance for widespread showers and storms arrives \nTuesday into Wednesday as the trailing cold front moves into and\nstalls over the state. This period will need to be monitored \nfor severe potential, as the upper-level jet extends into the \nGreat Lakes, increasing deep-layer shear along the stalled \nfront. Heavy rain will also be a concern with repeated rounds of\nstorms tracking east along the front. Through Wednesday night, \nNBM mean QPF shows one inch along and north of a Beardstown to \nBloomington line with the two inch contour clipping our far \nnorthwest forecast area near Galesburg.\n\n...Thursday and Friday...\nUnsettled conditions look to continue late next week as the \nbaroclinic zone remains draped across the Midwest, providing a \nfocus for daily rain chances. Guidance diverges on the precise \nlocation of the boundary by Thursday and especially Friday. \nAlthough specific timing and positioning details remain \nuncertain, daily chances for showers and thunderstorms will \npersist near the front. Temperatures will depend heavily on \nwhere the front settles, but current guidance favors highs from \nthe 70s north of the front to the upper 80s or low 90s south.",
